# Component Biome
#
# Components as features of the land rather than instances of a class: arising
# where conditions allow, developing presence with age, and exchanging with
# neighbours where the exchange pays. Light comes from the theme, position
# decides who gets it, and nothing associates in soil this rich.

#>component_biome
#:convention #!components #!ecology #!css
#:operation #!prime
#:fixity #!experimental
#:layer #!pragmatics

@region_ecology: ~"./region-component-ecology.spw"
@kernel_rhizosphere: ~"./kernel-rhizosphere.spw"
@stylesheet_ecology: ~"./stylesheet-ecology.spw"
@compound_expressions: ~"./compound-expressions.spw"
@arrival_electrostatics: ~"./arrival-electrostatics.spw"
@electrostatic_affordances: ~"./electrostatic-affordances.spw"
@component_taxonomy: ~"./component-taxonomy.spw"
@image_theming: ~"../../public/css/systems/image-theming.css"
@paper_motif: ~"../../public/css/systems/paper-motif.css"
@grain: ~"../../public/css/effects/grain-texture.css"

operation = "prime"
fixity = "experimental"

expression = component[biome]{light.exchange.succession}<land>

^"soil_is_too_rich"{
  claim: "Mycorrhizal association is conditional. In high-nutrient soil a plant reduces the symbiosis, because the carbon it costs is not worth the phosphorus it returns. The CSS here is high-nutrient soil, which is why no exchange has evolved in it."
  measured: .{
    custom_property_declarations = 6430
    has_selectors = 197
    container_queries = 42
    downward_to_upward_ratio = "33:1"
  }[reg=facet]
  reading: "6430 declarations flow down by inheritance, free and unlimited, available to everything. 197 `:has()` flow up. Exchange is 3% of the traffic, and it is 3% because nothing needs it — a component that can inherit whatever it wants has no reason to trade."
  consequence: "Scarcity is the precondition, not the goal. Until some token is not simply available, association cannot pay for itself and components will keep behaving as instances rather than as organisms."
  probe: `find public/css -name "*.css" -not -path "*/bundles/*" -exec grep -o ":has(" {} \; | wc -l`
}

^"the_only_upward_channel"{
  claim: "`:has()` is the sole bidirectional mechanism CSS currently offers. Everything else — inheritance, custom properties, container queries, style queries — reads downward."
  consequence: "Any exchange model has to be built on it. A region that changes because of what grew in it is a `:has()` selector; there is no second way to say that today."
  present: #[
    `@paper_motif — html:has(body[data-spw-page-family]) selects the motif from what the page declares`,
    `@image_theming — :has(img) gates the tint plate so an empty figure costs no layer`,
    `@arrival_electrostatics — the wall exists because the seat holds something`
  ][reg=set]
  gap: "All three are a parent reading its own children. None is a component contributing something a *sibling* can use, which is what a mycelial network actually does — resources moving between organisms that are not nested."
}

^"light"{
  claim: "Theme packs are the light source and already work. What is missing is canopy: every region receives full light regardless of where it sits."
  present: "Seven packs cast brightness, contrast, saturation and tint. @image_theming carries it to imagery; @paper_motif drives substrate opacity and blend, multiply on paper packs and screen on dark ones because multiply against a dark field erases the plate. @grain holds ambient field memory and an atmosphere veil."
  missing: "Shade. In a biome the canopy decides the understory, and position is most of what a species has to work with. Here a card in a dense cluster and a card alone in a wide frame receive identical light."
  shape: "Regional light should attenuate with what sits above and around — density, depth, and the shells @arrival_electrostatics already bands. A deep-band region under a dense cluster is understory and should read as understory."
  refuse: "Do not add a light token per component. One room loudness, per @electrostatic_affordances — canopy is a property of position, not a setting a card carries."
}

^"presence_by_age"{
  claim: "Grass and fungus differ in strategy, not only in appearance. Fast, dense, ruderal colonisers versus slow, networked, persistent ones. A component's presence should follow which it is behaving like."
  ruderal: "Arrives quickly, fills available space, tolerates disturbance, does not persist. Most cards. Presence should be immediate and cheap."
  networked: "Slow to establish, connects others, survives disturbance because the network does. The cauldron, the ledger, the expression kinship graph. Presence should accrue and should not reset on navigation."
  available_signal: "The site already measures age and traversal — ingredient phase runs gathering → resonant → mature → decayed, and expression salience bands 0..4 by dwell. Both are uptake by another name and neither currently changes how a component looks."
  next: "Let an established component read differently from a new one. That is succession, and the data for it is already stored."
}

^"exchange"{
  claim: "Mycorrhizal means bidirectional and costly. A component gives something to get something, and the association forms only where the trade pays."
  what_a_component_could_give: #[
    `its measured state — what it has learned about its own contents`,
    `its declared bonds — @compound_expressions, the authored coupling rather than the inferred one`,
    `its residue — the ledger entries it produced`
  ][reg=set]
  what_it_could_receive: #[
    `light it would not get from its position alone`,
    `material from a neighbour's region`,
    `salience earned by a kin it never hosted`
  ][reg=set]
  rule: "The exchange must cost. An association that is free is inheritance wearing a biological word, and the tree already has 6430 of those."
}

^"positioning_orchestration_join"{
  ~#note: "Extending the concurrent session's positioning-orchestration plan rather than duplicating it."
  affirm: "The batched MEASURE / PROJECT / AUDIT lifecycle is the right shape and fixes a real defect. Reading bounding rects during rAF and writing attributes in one mutation cycle is exactly what the j/k traversal added this week does not do — it reads offsetWidth over ~199 candidates on every keypress, which is the freeze pattern already recorded for this codebase."
  supplies: "That plan measures what canopy needs. Density here is currently structural — `:has(> :nth-child(6))` counts children, which is all CSS can see unaided. Real canopy is occlusion, not count."
  wired: "systems/component-biome.css already reads `--spw-edge-proximity` and `data-spw-extent` with inert fallbacks, so canopy becomes measured the moment the orchestrator projects them, with nothing else changing."
  divergence: "The plan's frame is a symphony — movements, orchestration, a score. A biome has no score and no conductor; structure emerges from organisms responding to local conditions and exchanging where the exchange pays. Both are coherent, and they imply different architectures: an orchestrator is a central authority that decides for its parts, which is the opposite of components arising and evolving."
  resolution: "Keep the batching, drop the conducting. A shared MEASURE pass is a soil survey — everyone benefits and nobody is told what to do with it. What each component does with its measured position should stay local, or the ecology is a puppet show."
  missing_pass: "MEASURE, PROJECT and AUDIT are all forward. There is no RETURN. 18 persistent stores accumulate and only the cauldron prunes — and pruning discards rather than composts, so nothing is fed by what is removed. A biome without decomposers buries itself in its own residue."
}

#>wonder_component_biome_1
?["What would have to become scarce for components to start trading?"]{
  #:depth #!mathematical ~#lens(nutrient)
  ~#tension: "every proposal for exchange founders on abundance — a component can already inherit any of 6430 declarations, so there is nothing it needs badly enough to give something up for. inventing scarcity artificially is a game mechanic, not an ecology."
  ~#hypothesis: "attention is the scarce nutrient and always has been. it is finite per visit, it is already measured (dwell, salience, arrival budget), and components genuinely compete for it — unlike tokens, which do not run out."
  !probe{ "count what a single page's components collectively demand of a reader against what one visit can supply — arrival discharges, salience deposits, feature encounters. if demand exceeds a visit, scarcity exists already and is simply unpriced." }
  $%[page.componentCount, visit.dwellTotal, salience.deposits, arrival.discharges]
}

#>wonder_component_biome_2
?["Does a region shade its neighbours, or only its children?"]{
  #:depth #!computational ~#lens(canopy)
  ~#tension: "`:has()` lets a parent read its descendants, so a container can shade what it contains. nothing lets a sibling shade a sibling, which is what canopy actually does — the tall grass beside you takes your light, not the grass above you, because there is nothing above you."
  ~#hypothesis: "sibling shading is expressible through the parent — a container that has a dense child can dim its other children — but it costs a selector per relationship and does not generalise, which is why nobody writes it."
  !probe{ "write sibling attenuation for one real cluster with :has() and count the selectors. if it takes more than three, the mechanism is wrong rather than the idea." }
  $%[region.density, region.depth, sibling.attenuation]
}

^"validation"{
  probe: `find public/css -name "*.css" -not -path "*/bundles/*" -exec grep -o ":has(" {} \; | wc -l`
  falsification: #[
    `An exchange is added that costs the giver nothing.`,
    `A component carries its own light token rather than reading position.`,
    `Succession is simulated with a timer rather than read from stored age or traversal.`
  ][reg=set]
  next: "Price attention on one page — count what its components demand against what a visit supplies. Scarcity has to be shown before exchange can be designed."
}
